Each scenario with its verified fix:

```tsx
// 1. MULTI-STEP FORM — state above the routes, or the URL is the state
// (a) lift: a context/store wrapping the wizard route
function WizardLayout() {
  const [model, setModel] = useState(initialModel);   // lives ABOVE the steps
  return (
    <RouteContext.Provider value={{ model, setModel }}>
      <Outlet />   {/* /wizard/step1, /wizard/step2 — all steps share model */}
    </RouteContext.Provider>
  );
}
// (b) durable: search params survive refresh + back — the draft IS the URL
const [params, setParams] = useSearchParams();
setParams({ step: 'billing', plan: 'pro' });   // /wizard?step=billing&plan=pro

// 2. INVALID DYNAMIC ID — not-found is a UI STATE, not a crash
const { id } = useParams();
const { data, status } = useQuery(['item', id], () => api.item(id));
// render: loading | error-with-back-to-list | content — never data?.name on undefined
if (status === 'error') return <NotFound message="Item doesn't exist" backTo="/items" />;

// 3. REFRESH 404 — the SPA fallback (the server must serve index.html for ALL routes)
// nginx: try_files $uri /index.html;
// vercel.json / netlify.toml: { "rewrites": [{ "source": "(.*)", "destination": "/index.html" }] }
```

The reasoning per family (the interview structure):
1. Route changes remount the route's subtree — a step component's state is scoped to its mount. The two fixes differ in DURABILITY: lifted state survives navigation within the session; search-param state survives refresh and is shareable (a support agent can open the exact step). Prefer URL state for the parts that make sense as a link (step, plan, filters), store data for the rest.
2. A dynamic segment (items/:id) makes the router hand you ANY string — 'abc' when ids are numbers, a deleted id, a crafted junk id. The discipline: treat the fetch result as three states and design the error state as a real UI (message + way back), not a thrown exception mid-render.
3. This is the most common 'works in dev, broken in prod' router issue: dev servers always fall back; static hosts serve files only. The fix is host config, not React — name the host you know (nginx try_files, vercel rewrites, netlify redirects, express ​app.get('*') → sendFile(index.html)) and you pass this question cold.
